Reviewing what have been done yesterday, yesterday when I wanted to leave, I would like to save what I have done with the git push but I were not sure it's ok or not.
Today reviewing...
pwd, to know where I am
ls, to list all the contents 
cd + folder name, go into it
ls, to see what's there
git status, how the status
  So here I see that yesterday I didn't do the "commit"
git commit -m "added ex00 ex01 ex02 exercises done", so here only with the git commit, I do commited it to git.
git push, and then with the push I updated it to the repository.
git status, again check the status

So here when I asked Claude what's more I need to do, Claude suggested me to do the following:
  git add hi.txt
  git commit -m "remove hi.txt test file"
  git push
Confused, because I didn't want to add hi.txt. 
Configured out: 
  git add, doesn't mean "add the file", in git, git add really menas "stage this change", whatever that change is, creation, modification, or deletion.
  so here git add hi.txt, since hi.txt has been deleted, running git add hi.txt tells git "yes, stage this deletion, I want it recorded."
  another way to do the same:
    git rm hi.txt, this will do 2 things at once: deletes the file from disk (if it's still there), and stage ths deletion for commit.
Ok, now I have finished the unfinished part from yesterday. Continue with today's part.

