Exercise 03: SSH
SSH Key
Turn-in directory: ex03/
Files to turn in: id_rsa_pub
Allowed functions: None
Create your own SSH key. Once done:
◦ Add your public key to your repository in a file named id_rsa_pub
◦ Update your SSH key on the intranet. This will allow you to push the repository to our git server

************
Ok, it comes back to yesterday's video 2, creation of SSH code
ssh-keygen
cd ~/.ssh
ls

So yesterday I have done the ssh-keygen once, and saved the public key in the intra network, so the private key should be saved as well in my computer, ~/.ssh/
I can look it up with:
  ls -la ~/.ssh
  id_rsa and id_rsa.pub are in pair, the one with "pub" is public and without "pub" is the private key.
If i run ssh-keygen again, it will generate a new pair of keys.

I planed to copy and paste the public key and do it with:
  echo -n "paste here the public key" > id_rsa_pub
But Claude suggested me to do it with "cp" or "cat >>" instead because copy and paste is risky to lose some characters. Agree.

I did it wrong, as I wanted to copy the public key in id_rsa.pub to a new file called id_rsa_pub, but I named it id_rsa.pub again, so now I need to rename it.
mv (move) is the command for it
  mv id_rsa.pub id_rsa_pub
  then use ls -la to check it
or if not in the current folder, use:
  mv ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub ~/.ssh/id_rsa_pub

If I want to save it and commit it, push it with frequency, I always need to follow the steps:
cd (into the folder)
git add
git commit -m "  "
git push
git status

