Exercise 05: git commit
Turn-in directory: ex05/
Files to turn in: git_commit.sh
Allowed functions: None

Create a shell script that displays the ids of the last 5 commits in your git repository.
%> bash git_commit.sh | cat -e
baa23b54f0adb7bf42623d6d0a6ed4587e11412a$
2f52d74b1387fa80eea844969e8dc5483b531ac1$
905f53d98656771334f53f59bb984fc29774701f$
5ddc8474f4f15b3fcb72d08fcb333e19c3a27078$
e94d0b448c03ec633f16d84d63beaef9ae7e7be8$
%>
Your script will be tested in our own environment.

****************
Ok, let's separete the task into some parts, we need to display the logs, indicate the specific number of recent ids, save the command in a script, save the script in a folder.
git log -n 5 --format="%H", or git log -n 5 --format="%h"
  git log: to check the commit history
  -n 5: can also be -5 directly, limit to show 5 recent commit only, can modify the number if we want more recent commit history
  --format="%H": format, get the complete commit ID Hash, if it's "%h", then ony the first 7 digits of the Hash commit ID.

cat -e, what does it mean?
  in Linux/Unix, all - usually means a Flag/Option
  in cat, -e comes from Show End of Line, prints $ in each line end, means the line ends there, is where \n is. can also show invisible characters, like Tab or Enter.
important, because it can show the line has ended.
  For example, 
    Hello World$
    (there is a $, so the line ends normally)

    Hello World $
    (wrong, there is a space extra)

    Hello World 
    (wrong, without $ to end, missing \n)

I wanted to write like this:
  echo "git log -n 5 --format="%H"" > git_commit.sh
  but Linux will understand it wrong, with separate segments,"git log -n 5 --format=" + %H + ""
  so Claude suggested me to do it with '...' only:
  
echo 'git log -n 5 --format=%H' > git_commit.sh

and I don't understand the codes in the task:  bash git_commit.sh | cat -e, there is a | in the middle
|, pipe, takes the output of the command on the left and feeds it as input to the command on the right
cat -e → displays the input with line endings made visible as $ at the end of each line, and shows tabs as ^I

